## Why This Is Asked

This checks understanding of IND-CCA security and how to build practical, protocol-level defenses in modern cryptographic systems.

## Key Concepts

- IND-CCA and chosen-ciphertext resistance
- Encrypt-then-MAC vs MAC-then-Encrypt
- Nonce management, associated data, and MAC verification order
- Trade-offs: performance, ciphertext size, API surface

## Code Example

```javascript
function encrypt(plaintext, key) {
  const nonce = crypto.randomBytes(12);
  const cipher = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-128-gcm', key, nonce);
  const enc = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);
  const tag = cipher.getAuthTag();
  const payload = Buffer.concat([nonce, enc, tag]);
  const mac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key).update(payload).digest();
  return { payload, mac };
}

function decrypt(payload, mac, key) {
  if (!verifyMac(mac, payload, key)) throw new Error('MAC fail');
  const nonce = payload.slice(0, 12);
  const enc = payload.slice(12, -16);
  const tag = payload.slice(-16);
  const decipher = crypto.createDecipheriv('aes-128-gcm', key, nonce);
  decipher.setAuthTag(tag);
  return Buffer.concat([decipher.update(enc), decipher.final()]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you audit for decryption oracle leakage?
- How would you enforce constant-time MAC checks and retry limits to prevent side channels?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical DP budget enforcement in a real API, including idempotency and budget rollover.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy budget accounting
- Idempotent API design
- Month rollover handling
- Auditability and logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: debit epsilon atomically per compute
function debit(userId, month, eps) {
  const b = budgets[`${userId}:${month}`];
  if ((b.remaining -= eps) < 0) throw 'Budget exhausted';
  return b.remaining;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle concurrent debits on the same budget?
- How to test idempotency with duplicate request_ids?

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges how well a candidate designs feature-level gating in a per-user scoring API, a practical knob for privacy, performance, and experimentation.

## Key Concepts
- Feature gating: filter features at the API boundary.
- Deterministic scoring: gating should not introduce nondeterminism.
- Validation & observability: log gating decisions for audits.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply feature gating before scoring
function computeScoreWithGating(allFeatures, allowedFeatures){
  const mask = new Set(allowedFeatures || allFeatures.map(f => f.name));
  const included = allFeatures.filter(f => mask.has(f.name));
  return computeScoreFromFeatures(included);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to validate and handle invalid feature names?
- How would you scale gating across multiple model_versions?
